    "created_at": "2017-08-04T05:41:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-08-04T05:42:35Z",
    "body": "I'm guessing the issue here was probably that the original events in HTML 4.01 were nouns and were prefixed by \"on\":\r\n\r\nhttps://www.w3.org/TR/html401/interact/scripts.html#h-18.2.3\r\n\r\n* onload\r\n* onunload\r\n* onclick\r\n* ondblclick\r\n* onmousedown\r\n* onmouseup\r\n* onmouseover\r\n* onmousemove\r\n* onmouseout\r\n* onfocus\r\n* onblur\r\n* onkeypress\r\n* onkeydown\r\n* onkeyup\r\n* onsubmit\r\n* onreset\r\n* onselect\r\n* onchange\r\n\r\nThe ambiguity of their part of speech led to them being reanalyzed as verbs and separated from the 'on' prefix.\r\n\r\nOther events, like the ones you mentioned, probably originated in other specs before being incorporated into HTML, which meant that they had different motivations and usage contexts that influenced their naming.\r\n\r\nIn addition, I don't think it's tense that's at play here at all, but [aspect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grammatical_aspect). Event names are inherently without [tense](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grammatical_tense).\r\n\r\nAs far as the historical timeline is concerned, this is primarily speculation on my part. But I would look to see where these events originated before they were incorporated into the broader definition of HTML.",
